<edge source="us_china_rivalry::geopolitical_tension_lev" target="energy_market_shift::sanction_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising US–China strategic tension leads policymakers and allied governments to expand and coordinate targeted economic measures and secondary sanctions against firms and transactions linked to the other side, directly increasing international sanction pressure on energy deals.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">energy_market_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_rivalry::geopolitical_tension_lev" target="energy_market_shift::control_share_reserves" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">As US–China rivalry intensifies, host governments implement restrictions, renegotiations or reassignments that reduce the effective share of proven reserves under external actors' control to secure domestic strategic autonomy.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">energy_market_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_rivalry::geopolitical_tension_lev" target="global_financial_tension::portfolio_realloc_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Acute rises in geopolitical tension (e.g., sanctions, military incidents, diplomatic escalation) trigger rapid risk-off behavior and cross-asset selling by global investors, driving accelerated reallocations into perceived safe assets and away from exposed equities and emerging-market positions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_financial_tension</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_rivalry::geopolitical_tension_lev" target="iran_war_stalemate::shipping_disruption" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising US–China strategic tensions drive greater naval presence, surveillance and contestation around key sea lanes and chokepoints, raising the probability of interdictions, inspections, and insurance/operational interruptions that disrupt shipping serving the Iran theater.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_war_stalemate</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_rivalry::geopolitical_tension_lev" target="iran_war_stalemate::oil_export_constraint" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating great‑power rivalry increases diplomatic pressure and coordinated administrative measures (port denials, reflagging pressure, insurance restrictions) among allied partners that effectively constrain or slow Iranian oil exports.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_war_stalemate</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_rivalry::geopolitical_tension_lev" target="russia_ukraine_war::commodity_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased U.S.–China rivalry raises trade frictions, shipping risk premia and insurance costs and prompts precautionary stockpiling and rerouting, which together push up global prices for staple commodities (grains and related agricultural commodities).</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
